><contents-richtext
><body xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" xmlns:xfa="http://www.xfa.org/schema/xfa-data/1.0/" xfa:APIVersion="Acrobat:25.1.0" xfa:spec="2.0.2"
><p dir="ltr"
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-14.0pt;text-align:left;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>Q14c: </span
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-12.7pt;text-align:left;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>This study provided insights into coaches’ perspectives of skill&#xD;acquisition in elite freestyle swimming. It is evident that swimming&#xD;coaches view swimming as a complex motor skill that&#xD;requires the invariant repetition of a movement pattern (Seifert&#xD;et al., </span
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-12.7pt;text-align:left;color:#00007F;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>2014</span
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-12.7pt;text-align:left;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>). Thus, designing practice tasks to enhance skill&#xD;learning is viewed as a balancing act between protecting the&#xD;confidence of the athletes, by providing environments that&#xD;enable them to be successful, versus exposing them to more&#xD;demanding tasks or situations where they might be less successful&#xD;(Renshaw et al., </span
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-12.7pt;text-align:left;color:#00007F;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>2009</span
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-12.7pt;text-align:left;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>). The prescription of training&#xD;practices that progress the swimming stroke from basic to full&#xD;coordination, or decompose the stroke into component parts&#xD;were common approaches used to develop skill among the&#xD;swimming coaches sampled. Participants also indicated the&#xD;use of constraint manipulations (e.g., swimming with&#xD;a parachute) to better facilitate transfer of learning. The participant&#xD;responses indicated that swimming coaches seem to&#xD;intuitively use variants of the constraints-led approach in their&#xD;practice design, yet they may be unaware of the theoretical&#xD;context behind using it (Renshaw et al., </span
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-12.7pt;text-align:left;color:#00007F;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>2019</span
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-12.7pt;text-align:left;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>). The recent&#xD;interactions coaches had with a skill acquisition consultant&#xD;may have helped shape the implementation of such&#xD;approaches in practice. Further empirical research is required&#xD;to determine the positive (or negative) effect that the common&#xD;training tasks have on skill learning, transfer, and performance.&#xD;Regardless, the experiential knowledge from coaches provides&#xD;insights into swimming high-performance training programmes&#xD;in Australia and can guide future research protocols&#xD;to better facilitate the transfer of empirical findings to the&#xD;performance environment (Greenwood et al., </span
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-12.7pt;text-align:left;color:#00007F;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>2014</span
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-12.7pt;text-align:left;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>).</span
></p
></body
